        "The internet is a vast network that links billions of computers and devices so they can share information almost instantly. It began in the 1960s as a research project to help separate computer networks communicate.",
        "To let very different machines exchange data reliably, engineers agreed on shared rules called protocols. Because information is broken into small packets that can travel along many possible paths, the network is flexible and hard to shut down completely.",
        "In the 1990s, the World Wide Web made the internet easy to use by linking documents through clickable pages. Web browsers and search engines soon allowed ordinary people to find information quickly.",
        "Today the internet supports communication, education, business, and entertainment. People send messages, attend classes, shop, and watch videos from nearly anywhere.",
        "This power brings new challenges, including questions about privacy, the accuracy of information, and unequal access. Using the internet wisely means thinking carefully about both its benefits and its risks.",
